From 13b9a52cd0e0283e613254cb46bd987b43d8886e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ebben Aries <exa@dscp.org>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 11:59:59 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] feat(clock): timezone/calendar display improvements and
 native-tooltip rendering

Timezones:
- timezone/timezones entries can be an object {"zone": ..., "label": ...}
  to override the display name shown in {tz_list}, in addition to a plain
  zone-name string (label defaults to the IANA zone name, as before).
- native-tooltip (bool, default false): {tz_list} renders as a real
  Gtk::Grid instead of a padded plain-text block relying on a monospace
  font for alignment. Styled entirely via .clock-timezones/
  .clock-timezone-label/.clock-timezone-time CSS classes -- the backend
  does no formatting of its own. timezone-column-spacing/
  timezone-row-spacing control grid spacing (a GTK3 widget property, not
  something CSS can set).
- timezone-tooltip-format also accepts a list of format strings instead
  of one: each entry becomes its own grid column
  (.clock-timezone-col-0/-1/...) when native-tooltip is enabled, so a
  variable-width field like {:%Z} doesn't ragged-align the columns next
  to it. A single string keeps the original one-column behavior.
- Rows whose local calendar date differs from today's (a timezone far
  enough away to already be tomorrow, or still yesterday) get
  .clock-timezone-day-ahead/-behind on every cell in that row.

Calendar:
- native-tooltip also covers {calendar}, but only in month mode -- year
  mode keeps using the existing get_calendar() markup-string path
  unchanged. A Gtk::Stack toggles between the native grid and that
  fallback each tick based on the current mode, so switching modes at
  runtime doesn't require rebuilding the tooltip's widget tree.
  .clock-calendar/.clock-calendar-months/.clock-calendar-weekdays/
  .clock-calendar-days/.clock-calendar-today/.clock-calendar-weeks CSS
  classes; calendar-column-spacing/calendar-row-spacing config ints for
  the same GTK3 grid-spacing-isn't-CSS reason as the timezone grid.
  calendar.format.* templates are still honored for literal content
  (e.g. weeks' "W{}" prefix) but Pango markup tags are stripped in
  native mode, since presentation comes from the CSS classes instead.
  The month/year header is explicitly left-aligned (GTK3 Label
  alignment is a widget property, not CSS-settable, and defaults to
  centered). The weekday header's abbreviation width matches year
  mode's (weekdayAbbrev2(), shared with get_calendar()'s existing
  truncation logic) instead of the locale's untruncated %a, so the two
  modes agree when you switch between them.
- The year-mode fallback gets its own .clock-calendar-text class, since
  that path still aligns columns via character padding and so still
  needs a monospace font pinned explicitly -- style.css can no longer
  rely on an outer <span font=...> in tooltip-format for this, since a
  span wrapped around a natively-rendered placeholder gets torn apart
  once that placeholder is extracted into its own widget slot.

Implementation:
- tooltip-format is split into an ordered sequence of literal text
  segments and {calendar}/{tz_list} widget slots at construction, only
  when native-tooltip applies to at least one of them. The outer
  tooltip becomes a Gtk::Box holding these in order instead of a single
  markup Gtk::Label. get_calendar()'s row/line algorithm
  (cldRowsInMonth, cldGetWeekForLine, the getCalendarLine
  case-2-vs-default split for the first week row's leading blanks vs.
  subsequent rows) is reused as-is for date math in the native grid;
  updateCalGrid()/updateTzGrid() are parallel populate-a-widget
  versions of the same logic instead of parallel append-to-a-padded-
  string versions. Grid shape (row/column count) is rebuilt only when
  it actually changes (navigating to a different-length month, cycling
  timezones in/out of view), which already goes through doAction()'s
  forced requery.

Man page updated throughout: new options, new CSS classes, and
examples for labeled timezones, the native timezone/calendar grids, a
multi-column timezone format, and combining calendar+timezones in one
tooltip.

KNOWN ISSUE: query_tlp_cb() can rarely hand GTK's tooltip a
m_tooltipBox_ whose GTK parent state has diverged from GTK's internal
tooltip-window tracking, causing gtk_container_add() to abort (visible
under G_DEBUG=fatal-warnings; in normal operation it likely just logs
a non-fatal warning and the popup renders empty). Reproduced once via
a real screenshot+right-click sequence, backtrace captured, but not
yet understood well enough to fix correctly -- an attempted fix
(detaching the widget from its current parent before set_custom())
made this deterministic instead of rare, by bypassing GTK's own
custom-widget bookkeeping and leaving it permanently desynced after
the first successful display. That attempt has been reverted; this is
an open problem, not a resolved one.

Rebased onto 59d2f70e, which added getStandaloneMonthName() for the
text-mode calendar header because %B yields the genitive in many Slavic
and Baltic locales. The native month grid's header now calls it too,
instead of formatting {:L%B %Y} itself, so the two calendar modes agree
in those locales -- the same cross-mode consistency weekdayAbbrev2()
exists for.
