+When *native-tooltip* is enabled, *{tz_list}* (always) and *{calendar}* (in
+month mode only) are real GTK grids instead of plain text, and the following
+classes can be styled like any other widget (font, color, weight, padding,
+border, background, ...) -- nothing about their appearance is set by the
+backend. Any *color*/*<b>*/*<u>* markup in *calendar.format.\** is still
+applied to *{calendar}*'s content when it falls back to year mode, but is
+ignored for the native month-mode grid; literal text in those format strings
+(e.g. the *"W"* prefix in a *weeks* format like *"W{}"*) is kept:
+
+- *.clock-timezones*: The grid containing all timezone rows
+- *.clock-timezone-label*: Each row's label cell (the zone name or its
+  configured *label* override)
+- *.clock-timezone-time*: Each row's time cell(s). Present on every time cell
+  regardless of column count, for shared styling
+- *.clock-timezone-col-0*, *-1*, ...: Added alongside *.clock-timezone-time* on
+  each cell, one per *timezone-tooltip-format* entry, only when
+  *timezone-tooltip-format* has 2 or more entries. Lets a specific column (e.g.
+  a trailing *{:%Z}* abbreviation) be styled or aligned differently from its
+  neighbors
+- *.clock-timezone-day-ahead*, *.clock-timezone-day-behind*: Added to every
+  cell in a row (label and all time columns) when that timezone's local
+  calendar date is after/before yours. Neither class is present when it's the
+  same day. Since these share specificity with *.clock-timezone-label*/
+  *.clock-timezone-time*, declare them *after* those rules in *style.css* (or
+  use a more specific selector) so they actually take effect
+- *.clock-calendar*: The month's day grid
+- *.clock-calendar-months*: The month/year header, above the grid
+- *.clock-calendar-weekdays*: The weekday header row (Su, Mo, ...)
+- *.clock-calendar-days*: Each day-number cell
+- *.clock-calendar-today*: Added alongside *.clock-calendar-days* on the
+  current day's cell
+- *.clock-calendar-weeks*: Each week-number cell (only present when
+  *calendar.weeks-pos* is set)
+- *.clock-calendar-text*: The year-mode fallback (still the old padded-text
+  calendar, since native rendering is month-mode only for now). It still
+  relies on character-padding alignment, so it needs a monospace *font-family*
+  set here or it will misalign.
+
+```
+.clock-timezones {
+	border: 1px solid #444;
+	border-radius: 6px;
+	padding: 6px;
+}
+.clock-timezone-label {
+	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
+	font-weight: bold;
+	padding-right: 8px;
+}
+.clock-timezone-time {
+	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
+}
+.clock-calendar-months {
+	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
+	font-weight: bold;
+}
+.clock-calendar-today {
+	color: #ff6699;
+	text-decoration: underline;
+}
+```
